Splunk SIEM: SSH Brute-Force Detection

This lab demonstrates a complete SOC-level workflow using Splunk Enterprise to detect and analyze SSH brute-force attacks. 
A Kali Linux machine was configured as the log source, where /var/log/auth.log was monitored and forwarded to Splunk using 
the Splunk Universal Forwarder. The lab began by validating successful log ingestion and ensuring authentication events were 
indexed and searchable.
An attack simulation was performed using repeated failed SSH login attempts (ssh fakeuser@localhost), generating events such 
as Invalid user, multiple Failed password attempts, and connection closure. These logs were analyzed in Splunk to reconstruct 
the full attack timeline and identify key indicators, including the attacker IP (::1) and the targeted username (fakeuser).
Using SPL queries, fields such as src_ip and user were extracted with rex, and statistical analysis (stats, bin) was applied to 
correlate events and detect brute-force behavior based on frequency thresholds. Detection logic was validated by grouping failed 
attempts within a time window and identifying repeated activity from a single source.
The lab concluded with the creation of reports, alerts, and a SOC dashboard to visualize attack patterns, including failed logins 
by IP, attack timelines, and targeted usernames. This provided a functional SIEM use case for real-time monitoring and detection of 
SSH brute-force attacks.
